    {
      "id": "66",
      "type": "related_content",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 66,
        "relationship_type": "related",
        "body": "だろう is a form of だ used when the speaker feels uncertain. It's often translated as 'probably,' 'I guess,' or '...right?' when asking a question. In contrast, ね is used to seek confirmation or agreement, often translated as '...isn't it?' These two can combine to express both uncertainty and confirmation, as in 面白いだろうね ('It's probably interesting, isn't it?').",
        "first_relatable": {
          "id": 18,
          "slug": "だろう",
          "title": "だろう",
          "meaning": "Right?, Probably, Isn't it?",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        },
        "second_relatable": {
          "id": 71,
          "slug": "ね",
          "title": "ね",
          "meaning": "Isn't it?, Right? (Sentence ending particle) ",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "38",
      "type": "related_content",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 38,
        "relationship_type": "related",
        "body": "でしょう is a form of です used when the speaker is uncertain, often translated as 'probably,' 'I guess,' or '...right?' when asking a question. In contrast, ね is a sentence-ending particle used to seek confirmation or agreement, similar to '...isn't it?' When combined (e.g., 面白いでしょうね), the sentence expresses both uncertainty and a request for agreement: 'It's probably interesting, isn't it?'",
        "first_relatable": {
          "id": 9,
          "slug": "でしょう",
          "title": "でしょう",
          "meaning": "Right?, Probably, Isn't it?",
          "level": "JLPT5",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        },
        "second_relatable": {
